e comments  below if you are a new member of this  here  channel welcome so gege akitami author  of jujutsukai sen and self-depicted  one-eyed cat despite this depiction  akatami is actually a very empathetic  and understandable figure to me because  we're roughly the same age and we fell  in love with the world of anime and  manga during the same sort of era by  which i mean the age of what is called  the big  three being the titles of one piece  naruto and bleach which basically  dominated everything shonen and like  most authors akatami took a particular  liking to one of those three being  bleach  and the work of tita kubo in general in  addition to that akatami also had a lot  of love for the work of yoshihiro  tagashi  specifically citing you haksho and  hunter hunter which immediately gives us  a great idea of the subgenre that  jujutsu kaisen is really pushing forward  which is the realm of dark shonen  the sort of series that either deal with  intense subject matter more than your  feel good happy shonen series and other  examples of dark shonen include classics  like death note d  grey man or even attack on titan stuff  that really pushes the boundaries of  what can be done in terms of story  themes and artwork without turning into  a full-blown saint in series like  berserk  ants or villain saga although one can  argue that at times that line is  definitely  definitely crossed not quite today  though because jujutsu kaisen is quite  firmly ashonin and you can tell because  it adopts  many many key tropes whether that be the  high school setting the power of  friendship vibes all sorts of classic  protagonist parental issues and lately  in the manga we're even about to see the  series take on its own sort of  tournament arc and saying all of those  things one after the other in a listy  sort of format does sound rather cringey  and annoying because we know the  standard way in which all of those ideas  tend to play out but the brilliance  fakatami's work is how these tropes are  applied  so for example the high school setting  it plays almost no role in the events of  the actual manga  yes yuji and friends of yuji technically  study at a place called jiuutsuhai but  they've never been shown having any  classes or doing any typical high school  activities the closest we've come i  suppose would be the kyoto goodwill  event but other than that the fact that  they're high school students  is almost completely irrelevant really  studying at jujutsu high is more akin to  being a new employee at a company our  students are thrust straight into work  and don't have to deal with painful  complications of the high school setting  similarly judas kaisen is not immune  from the old power of friendship trope  you know the whole we're stronger  together and we can overcome all of the  anything sort of vibe however it is  utilized very differently akatami  actually goes to some pretty great  lengths to subvert the trope by putting  a huge emphasis on the fact that jude's  sorcerers are quintessentially lonely  existences and destined to die alone at  the same time however our main character  is trying to halt that trend by making  sure that he dies surrounded by others  so the power of friendship is much more  naturally applied and definitely not  anywhere near as in your face as like a  classic protagonist makes speech about  not being alone style  sort of thing and with yuji as well as  everyone else  the idea is very internalized ah and  speaking of internal  a big big trope that jutsukai sin taps  into is that of the inner demon  like a literal inner demon and this is  where yuji rips are paid straight out of  the book of ichigo but it's obviously  not an exclusive feature to bleach but i  feel like jujutsu kaisen is the first  series to really examine the oh damn  aspect of the inner demon trope like  there is no trusting sukuna whatsoever  there is no working with sukuna and he  may very well be the primary antagonist  of the entire series so the one thing i  really hope never happens is yuji and  sukun are having this moment of like a  permanent teamer in the exact same way  that does tend to happen with every  other inner demon in churning because  there is no redemption for tsukuna he's  done something some seriously messed up  stuff and there is very very good reason  why many people would rather have yuji  executed right here and now but further  bleach inspiration can also be seen with  the concept of domains which certainly  do have a bank is quality to them just  with a lot more rules and in many ways  they're a lot more overpowered  speaking of rules this is something that  jujutsu kaisen absolutely excels at it  sets rules for its world and its power  system and  then it does something even better which  is that it abides by those rules and i  know that sounds really simple  but it's it's really not and this starts  out at a very basic level by explaining  how to conjure cursed energy but it ends  up evolving into these wildly complex  situations featuring a litany of cursed  mechanics at play simultaneously as well  as the ever expanding domains and domain  countermeasures so i've made this  comparison before but the curse system  is extraordinarily evocative of the nen  system of hunter hunter but the whole  manifestation of x energy being  channeled into y  ability although i would say that  akutami has set fast stricter  limitations and togashi did but the  result is still  extremely effective absolutely anything  akatami wants to happen  can happen with a satisfactory  explanation and if you keep looking it  does fulfill other shonen tropes like  the absentee parents the glorious mascot  character that is panda  ah such a good character and so on and  so forth but equally as important is  what jujutsu kaisen chooses to emit  there are particular shonen features and  devices that akutami goes to great  lengths not to include in the series  one of which is fan service i think one  of the most wild thoughts i've ever had  about jiu jitsu kaisen was when i  finished my read-through and i realized  that i'd never seen kugisaki in a bikini  or her underwear  or in the shower or like anything  however many ways authors find to really  showcase the  the female form as well as the male form  actually but the same goes for other  female characters as well mai  maki miwa in fact i think the closest we  may have come to  fanservice is yugi's poster of jennifer  lawrence and maymay  sort of i mean she has this very  provocative volume cover but even with  her seductress style character she still  spends most of her time  very very dressed and i really can't  think of a single instance where we've  even come close to objectifying a female  character even takura whose role as an  idol is essentially to be objectified  and to me it just goes to prove  something that i've thought for a very  very long time which is that fan service  really doesn't  add much if any value to a series if  anything it just takes that valuable  time away from story and getting to know  these characters  similarly in jujutsu kaisen there is  next to no element of comical romance by  which i mean stuff like  say sanji from one piece fawning over  every woman in existence or even the  other way around like noel in black  clover being all  sunderer with astor and as a result the  characters just feel a lot more  like real and it's easier to be immersed  in the manga because nobody does  like cinderella yandere thing in real  life and nobody does it in shoot sky  sense so that immediately puts it one  step closer to reality than most shown  in series  and another thing that akhitami  generally leaves by the wayside is the  idea of big dreams this one gets talked  about  a lot you know luffy wants to become the  pirate king  astor wants to become the wizard king  and naruto wants to become what is  essentially the ninja king  ash wants to be the very best like no  one ever was as does hinata just with  volleyball instead  of legally sanctioned animal fights  whereas yuji  he he just wants to do a good thing for  the world and then die with some close  friends by his side fushiguru is driven  by an ideology the good people deserve a  chance to be saved and the most  simplistic of all is gugisaki whose  entire life to this point has been  guided by the desire to just meet her  old friend again that and eat nice  things in the city food is also  important they're almost stunningly  mundane goals for a trio of protagonists  and usually i would argue that this is a  bad detractive feature i personally do  believe that shonen tends to work best  when our main characters have grand  ambition  something that we can really get on  board with and experience their drive to  achieve it but then again when you  consider the world of jujitsu kaisen  these characters do have some pretty  grand goals take yuji he might not want  to become the cursed  king or whatever but his whole idea of  not dying alone is apparently one of the  most ridiculously difficult things to  achieve in this particular world and  that's because for a story about magical  curse powers it's pretty highly grounded  in reality  our characters for the most part have  very basic human desires that we can  identify with and because it is so hard  to achieve those desires it's almost the  equivalent of saying something like yeah  i am going to become the cursed king  because that's just how dystopic this  world is that even the very basics are a  far away dream  and another important feature that  akatami purposely neglects is the  concept of the status quo jujutsukai sen  has never and will  never feature a status quo a state where  things begin and eventually return  back to after a big event and you see  this all too often in many other major  shonen series where after some sort of  gigantic climactic arc perhaps even saga  everything just ends up being okay life  goes back to the way it was  and there are no enduring consequences  whereas jujitsukai sen is constantly in  a state of flux  events happen things change more events  happen and then more things  change consequences are an essential  element of the series  it feels very very natural less like  we're coming to the end of a written and  structured story arc and more like we're  just watching the world move forward on  its own  which is a thing that the world does we  can never go back to a certain point in  history after any particular global  event in the real world things just keep  changing  and evolving and i feel like jujutsu  kaisen really reflects that feeling and  i also appreciate that  judas kaisen rarely has what i would  call a correct answer  every action even those taken by our  protagonist is very much in question  from a moral perspective one of my  favorite examples of this would be from  the death painting aqua afterwards yuji  and kugisaki have to find their own ways  to contend with what they've just done  and this goes on to haunt yuji in  particular for a fair while as do most  of his actions really and even when our  characters are very much doing the right  thing in a particular circumstance the  way in which they perform those actions  can also leave  questions during extreme situations our  supposed good guys can really go down  quite a dark path with fushigura and  kogisaki in particular  being known for concerning maniacal  facial expressions  almost as if to state that they are  enjoying the sick battle far more than  anyone should  if indeed they should be enjoying it at  all and when these features are put  together it also leads to the omission  of another trope being sweet victory the  idea that yeah we beat the bad guy and  now we all get to celebrate and stuff  judit skysen does not operate like that  oftentimes even when we do achieve  victory it comes at a great cost  and there are several occasions where  victory essentially feels like the  equivalent of a loss  meanwhile all of the above and more is  the combined reason for why jujitsu  kaisen receives the insane praise it  does it's not because it necessarily  presents us with anything new  most of its key elements can be found on  mass in any battle shown in series but  what i think it does go on to prove is  that tropes are not  a bad thing unless they're used by  not so great writers tropes can be used  well they can be used very well  and it also pays to be very conscious of  which ones to employ and which ones to  not touch with a 10 meter cursed  tool pole that is jude skysen an  evolution of the shonen formula focusing  on what classic elements to work with  and innovative ways of using them as  well as identifying what classic  elements are probably a bit past their  expiration date  and may need to be retired from the  genre but now that you've watched this  video go  and watch more videos more of my videos  to be precise because there are things  things you will like  like this video so i look forward to  seeing you there

